      {
        "slide": 9,
        "role": "close",
        "subject": "A kit romanesque county courthouse at night, filling about half the width below the type. It has a stone and brick facade with red conical turret roofs and a clock tower, raked from the left so the turrets cast onto the facade. Several windows glow warm. A US and a Texas flag hang on a pole at the right. A resident stands on the walk with his back to the camera, and straw grass lies on both sides of the walk.",
        "visual_consequence": "At feed size a reader sees a lit county seat at night with a person facing it. The next step sits with a local public body, which is what the abatement condition means.",
        "weakness": "Minor kit artifacts. Windows at both corner turrets' inner edges are sliced in half where the turret geometry overlaps the facade. The resident stands still rather than walking and casts little visible shadow on the walk. Several windows are lit rather than the one planned. Brick and granite read as material at 100 percent, the turret casts are attached, and every base meets the ground.",
